{
  "requirement": "The `{term}` role followed by a backtick-delimited full definition name MUST reference a named definition. In rendered Markdown, the reference MUST display the definition's phrase as an ordinary hyperlink to its anchored declaration, without a badge. The phrase MUST be escaped appropriately for rendering.\n\nMissing targets and targets of another kind MUST be reported. Escaped references and references in Markdown code examples MUST remain literal. A term reference MUST NOT satisfy a requirement citation or a Rust function's requirement-coverage obligation.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is met. The `{impl}` code covers every obligation, and tests cover each of them. One minor marking gap remains (see the end).\n\n**Obligations and where they are implemented**\n- **A `{term}` role references a named definition.**\n  - `reference_roles` in `src/lib.rs` parses `{term}` followed by a backtick-delimited valid name into `ReferenceKind::Term`.\n  - It is marked `{impl}`.\n- **Rendering as an ordinary hyperlink, with no badge.**\n  - `replace_references` in `src/lib.rs` handles `Term` targets separately from impl/cite/test.\n  - It emits a plain `<a href=…>` whose text is the definition's phrase, and it never calls `shield_badge`.\n  - The href points to the anchored declaration. `replace_definitions` renders each term declaration as `<strong id=\"habeas-definition-…\">`, and `DefinitionKind::anchor` builds that id. Both are marked `{impl}`.\n  - `preprocess_book` applies the replacement to regular chapters and to generated detail pages.\n- **The phrase is escaped appropriately.** `terms::escaped_phrase` HTML-escapes the phrase and numerically escapes Markdown-significant characters (`* _ \\` [ ] \\\\ ~ { }`). It is marked `{impl}`.\n- **Missing targets and targets of another kind are reported.**\n  - In `validate_references` in `src/evidence.rs`, a `Term` reference whose target is missing or is not a `Term` produces \"term X has no matching named definition\".\n  - The inverse is also reported: `{impl}`, `{cite}` or `{test}` pointing at a term.\n  - On the book side, a non-matching term role is left literal.\n- **Escaped references and Markdown code examples stay literal.** `reference_roles` skips escaped positions and protected ranges. `protected_ranges` covers fenced code, inline code and HTML. It also protects the `:phrase:` option line of term declarations.\n- **A term reference never satisfies a citation or Rust coverage.**\n  - `validate_references` does not add term references to `by_definition`, so they cannot count as `{impl}`/`{test}` coverage.\n  - `missing_function_references` in `src/rust_coverage.rs` explicitly excludes `Term` roles and `Term` targets.\n  - `terms::referenced_terms` routes term references only to the dependency closure.\n\n**Tests** (all in `tests/definitions.rs` and marked `{test}`)\n- `invalid_declarations_and_reference_targets_are_reported`: a `{term}` pointing at a requirement (`rules.ready`) and one pointing at a nonexistent name (`missing.name`) each produce the \"has no matching named definition\" error.\n- `phrases_are_plain_links_and_markdown_literals_are_preserved`:\n  - The rendered link is a single `href=\"../glossary.html#habeas-definition-glossary.a\"` from a nested chapter.\n  - The output contains no `shields.io` badge.\n  - The phrase is escaped to `&#42;alpha&#42; &amp; &lt;beta&gt;`.\n  - Inline-code, fenced and backslash-escaped references stay unreplaced.\n  - The declaration carries the anchor id.\n- `outline_inputs_capture_terms_without_counting_them_as_citations`: a Rust function with only a `{term}` comment is reported as having \"no requirement or example citation\".\n- `definition_schema_deletion_and_literal_references_are_checked`: literal term references in inline code, a fence and an escape produce no validation errors and no inputs. A deleted glossary produces \"no matching named definition\".\n\n**Minor observation:** `chapter_href` and `escape_html` also contribute to term-link generation, but they are marked only for `requirements.index.navigation` and `requirements.references.safe_badges`. They are shared helpers, and the same convention applies to badge links, so I treat this as acceptable and not a material gap.",
